# cheap_goods_aid_events.dialogue
# Sample for trade-offs (cheap exports: short Cohesion boost, long factory erosion) and aid corruption (wasted into Hidden Hand).
# Launched from TradeErosion/AidCorruption states in chart or demo buttons. Makes building industry/Cohesion feel impactful with narrative choices and risks.

~ cheap_exports_choice

Cheap goods flood in from abroad via Trade Envoy deal. Your people celebrate short-term (public Cohesion surges – cheap stuff makes life easier now). But factories erode over time (deindustrialization narrative – long-term Mandate and industrial_base hit). Trade-off: Accept for immediate happiness or reject to protect long-term strength?

- Accept the Deal (Short Public Cohesion + , Long Erosion)
  do GameData.accept_cheap_exports_deal("trade_envoy", "AsiaPartner")
  People are happy now (riots down), but industrial base slowly decays. Hidden Hand may exploit eroded institutional Cohesion. Fun tension: Short-term relief vs long-term weakness.
  => end

- Reject and Protect Industry (No Short Boost, Preserve Base)
  do GameData.apply_pillar_shift("player", "mandate", 5, "protected_industry")
  No immediate Cohesion from cheap goods, but factories hold strong. Long-term Mandate gain from self-reliance. Trade-off: Harder now, stronger later.
  => end

=> END

~ aid_corruption_choice

You send aid to a struggling nation (or receive it). Short-term Cohesion boost for them (or your Mandate prestige if giving). But high risk of waste and corruption siphoned into Hidden Hand (erodes their elite/public groups, possible scandal hitting your Ascendancy). Narrative: Aid as tool or trap?

- Send/Accept Aid (Short Boost, Corruption Risk)
  do GameData.send_country_aid("aid_agent", "FailedState", 10)
  Short Cohesion or Mandate gain, but 60% chance wasted into Hidden Hand (groups eroded, scandals). Makes 'helping' a tense choice with real narrative depth (corruption, lost influence).
  => end

- Refuse Aid (No Boost, Avoid Risk)
  do GameData.apply_pillar_shift("player", "ascendancy", 3, "principled_stand")
  No short-term help, but avoid corruption trap. Hidden Hand less able to exploit. Trade-off: Miss opportunity, but cleaner long-term.
  => end

=> END

~ end

The choice has played out. Your pillars shift visibly – cheap goods or aid create short-term relief with long-term costs or Hidden Hand gains. Players feel the difference through agent decisions and trade-offs in the grand campaign.